{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01D6p5amULbkS5mGWV8p8BiY","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\n2\t\n3\t\\usepackage[review]{acl}\n4\t\n5\t\\usepackage{times}\n6\t\\usepackage{latexsym}\n7\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n8\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n9\t\\usepackage{microtype}\n10\t% \\usepackage{inconsolata} % unavailable locally; re-enable for submission\n11\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n12\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n13\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n14\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n15\t\\usepackage{subcaption}\n16\t\\usepackage{tikz}\n17\t\\usetikzlibrary{arrows.meta,positioning}\n18\t\n19\t\\title{The Likert Blindspot: Detecting Hidden Style Bias\\\\in LLM Judges via Controlled Style Decomposition}\n20\t\n21\t\\author{Anonymous}\n22\t\n23\t\\begin{document}\n24\t\\maketitle\n25\t\n26\t\\input{sections/abstract}\n27\t\n28\t\\input{sections/introduction}\n29\t\n30\t\\input{sections/related_work}\n31\t\n32\t\\input{sections/method}\n33\t\n34\t\\input{sections/experiments}\n35\t\n36\t\\input{sections/conclusion}\n37\t\n38\t\\section*{Limitations}\n39\t\\label{sec:limitations}\n40\t\n41\t\\paragraph{Quality--style confounding and construct validity.}\n42\tThe NLI gate blocks content changes but cannot block quality perception shifts mediated by style itself---a fundamental identifiability limit of text-rewriting approaches.\n43\tAlthough the formality effect survives logistic adjustment for original preference ($\\text{OR} = 2.32$, $p = 0.006$; Appendix~\\ref{app:direction}), residual confounding is not fully eliminated.\n44\tAn alternative interpretation of the Blindspot is that Likert and pairwise modes measure different constructs: Likert captures absolute quality difference while pairwise captures ordinal preference. Under this view, the divergence reflects construct difference rather than measurement failure. The Comparative Trap \\citep{jeong2025comparative} lends support to this alternative; we address it in \\S\\ref{sec:amplification} but cannot fully exclude format-level amplification without human ground-truth ratings.\n45\t\n46\t\\paragraph{Evaluator-producer bias and generalizability.}\n47\tQwen3-32B serves as both rewriter and judge, raising self-preference concerns \\citep{panickssery2024selfpreference}.\n48\tBT estimates are robust (cross-judge gap $+4.5$pp at $n = 355$), and GPT-4o independently confirms the Blindspot; however, cross-judge effect-size comparisons are not valid.\n49\tRegister results carry the highest evaluator-producer risk and should be treated as exploratory.\n50\tAll experiments use LLMBar ($n = 100$); additional axes, larger benchmarks, and further judges (Gemini, Llama) are needed.\n51\tWhether findings transfer to settings with longer outputs, domain-specific evaluation, or human judges requires further investigation.\n52\t\n53\t\\paragraph{Validation methodology and scale design.}\n54\tNLI validation uses Qwen3-32B as annotator (GPT-4o cross-validation on all 200 pairs confirms 92.1\\% formality / 82.6\\% verbosity pass precision); human evaluation of both original and rewritten texts would strengthen content-preservation claims.\n55\tWhether finer-grained scales \\citep{preston2000scale} could mitigate the Blindspot remains open; equivalence testing \\citep{lakens2017equivalence} could formalize whether the Likert effect size is practically zero.\n56\t\n57\t\\section*{Code and Data}\n58\tCode and data will be released upon acceptance.\n59\t\n60\t\\input{sections/appendix}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"67522f31-a5d0-4ea0-acb8-8470b668e7b1","uuid":"1843e6ae-3a1c-4234-8619-709f27dfa74a","timestamp":"2026-05-21T14:00:03.837Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0514_5/projects/causal_style_decomp_judge/docs/paper/main.tex","content":"\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\n\n\\usepackage[review]{acl}\n\n\\usepackage{times}\n\\usepackage{latexsym}\n\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage{microtype}\n% \\usepackage{inconsolata} % unavailable locally; re-enable for submission\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{subcaption}\n\\usepackage{tikz}\n\\usetikzlibrary{arrows.meta,positioning}\n\n\\title{The Likert Blindspot: Detecting Hidden Style Bias\\\\in LLM Judges via Controlled Style Decomposition}\n\n\\author{Anonymous}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\input{sections/abstract}\n\n\\input{sections/introduction}\n\n\\input{sections/related_work}\n\n\\input{sections/method}\n\n\\input{sections/experiments}\n\n\\input{sections/conclusion}\n\n\\section*{Limitations}\n\\label{sec:limitations}\n\n\\paragraph{Quality--style confounding and construct validity.}\nThe NLI gate blocks content changes but cannot block quality perception shifts mediated by style itself---a fundamental identifiability limit of text-rewriting approaches.\nAlthough the formality effect survives logistic adjustment for original preference ($\\text{OR} = 2.32$, $p = 0.006$; Appendix~\\ref{app:direction}), residual confounding is not fully eliminated.\nAn alternative interpretation of the Blindspot is that Likert and pairwise modes measure different constructs: Likert captures absolute quality difference while pairwise captures ordinal preference. Under this view, the divergence reflects construct difference rather than measurement failure. The Comparative Trap \\citep{jeong2025comparative} lends support to this alternative; we address it in \\S\\ref{sec:amplification} but cannot fully exclude format-level amplification without human ground-truth ratings.\n\n\\paragraph{Evaluator-producer bias and generalizability.}\nQwen3-32B serves as both rewriter and judge, raising self-preference concerns \\citep{panickssery2024selfpreference}.\nBT estimates are robust (cross-judge gap $+4.5$pp at $n = 355$), and GPT-4o independently confirms the Blindspot; however, cross-judge effect-size comparisons are not valid.\nRegister results carry the highest evaluator-producer risk and should be treated as exploratory.\nAll experiments use LLMBar ($n = 100$); additional axes, larger benchmarks, and further judges (Gemini, Llama) are needed.\nWhether findings transfer to settings with longer outputs, domain-specific evaluation, or human judges requires further investigation.\n\n\\paragraph{Validation methodology and scale design.}\nNLI validation uses Qwen3-32B as annotator (GPT-4o cross-validation on all 200 pairs confirms 92.1\\% formality / 82.6\\% verbosity pass precision); human evaluation of both original and rewritten texts would strengthen content-preservation claims.\nWhether finer-grained scales \\citep{preston2000scale} could mitigate the Blindspot remains open; equivalence testing \\citep{lakens2017equivalence} could formalize whether the Likert effect size is practically zero.\n\n\\section*{Code and Data}\nCode and data will be released upon acceptance.\n\n\\input{sections/appendix}","numLines":60,"startLine":1,"totalLines":65}}}
