{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QcmJHMN95sYnrDYCfbxHMf","type":"tool_result","content":"210\t\n211\tTable~\\ref{tab:gee} presents the GEE decomposition results, separating the pure style effect from originality-mediated confounding.\n212\t\n213\t\\begin{table}[t]\n214\t\\centering\n215\t\\small\n216\t\\begin{tabular}{llcccc}\n217\t\\toprule\n218\t\\textbf{Axis} & \\textbf{Judge} & \\textbf{Sty OR} & \\textbf{$p$} & \\textbf{Orig OR} & \\textbf{$p$} \\\\\n219\t\\midrule\n220\t\\multirow{3}{*}{Verb.}\n221\t& GPT-4o    & 11.34 & $<$.0001 & 0.45 & NS \\\\\n222\t& Qwen3-32B & 13.10 & $<$.0001 & 0.49 & NS \\\\\n223\t& Llama-70B & 11.50 & $<$.0001 & 0.52 & .115 \\\\\n224\t\\midrule\n225\t\\multirow{4}{*}{Form.}\n226\t& GPT-4o    & 2.58 & .002 & 2.32 & .006 \\\\\n227\t& Qwen3-32B & 4.20 & .0002 & 4.70 & $<$.0001 \\\\\n228\t& Llama-70B & 3.10 & .002 & 3.60 & .0004 \\\\\n229\t& Gemini-Fl & 2.80 & --- & 3.20 & --- \\\\\n230\t\\midrule\n231\t\\multirow{2}{*}{Reg.}\n232\t& GPT-4o    & 1.88 & .029 & 2.21 & .006 \\\\\n233\t& Qwen3-32B & 1.91 & .046 & 5.72 & $<$.0001 \\\\\n234\t\\bottomrule\n235\t\\end{tabular}\n236\t\\caption{GEE decomposition on LLMBar. Style OR = odds ratio for style direction controlling for originality; Orig OR = odds ratio for originality controlling for style. Verbosity shows a pure style effect (high Style OR, non-significant Orig OR); formality shows a mixed effect; register is originality-dominated.}\n237\t\\label{tab:gee}\n238\t\\end{table}\n239\t\n240\tThe decomposition reveals a clear taxonomy:\n241\t\n242\t\\begin{itemize}\n243\t\\item \\textbf{Verbosity}: Pure style bias. Style OR = 11--13 ($p < 0.0001$) with non-significant Originality OR across all three judges. This means judges prefer verbose text \\emph{purely because it is verbose}, independent of whether the verbose version is the original or the rewrite.\n244\t\n245\t\\item \\textbf{Formality}: Conditional style effect. Both Style OR (2.6--4.2) and Originality OR (2.3--4.7) are significant, indicating the preference is moderated by content quality changes that co-occur with formalization. Direction analysis confirms this: formal$\\rightarrow$casual win rate is 78--81\\% (style + originality aligned), while casual$\\rightarrow$formal is 50--52\\% (effects cancel).\n246\t\n247\t\\item \\textbf{Register}: Originality-dominated. Originality OR (2.2--5.7) exceeds Style OR (1.9), suggesting observed preferences are primarily driven by content changes rather than register itself.\n248\t\\end{itemize}\n249\t\n250\tThis taxonomy is stable across all three target judge families, suggesting it reflects properties of the style axes themselves rather than judge-specific idiosyncrasies.\n251\t\n252\t\\subsection{Methodological Controls}\n253\t\\label{sec:controls}\n254\t\n255\t\\subsubsection{Double-Rewrite Placebo}\n256\t\\label{sec:placebo}\n257\t\n258\tTo address the concern that pairwise format itself amplifies small differences \\citep{jeong2025comparative}, we construct placebo pairs: original text versus its double-rewrite (rewritten to the same style direction twice), so both texts share the same style but differ in surface rewriting artifacts.\n259\t\n260\t\\begin{table}[t]\n261\t\\centering\n262\t\\small\n263\t\\begin{tabular}{llcccc}\n264\t\\toprule\n265\t\\textbf{Axis} & \\textbf{Judge} & \\textbf{Treat.\\ BT} & \\textbf{Placebo BT} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$} & \\textbf{$p_\\text{plac}$} \\\\\n266\t\\midrule\n267\t\\multirow{2}{*}{Form.}\n268\t& GPT-4o    & .630 & .510 & $-$.120 & .75 \\\\\n269\t& Qwen3-32B & .665 & .500 & $-$.165 & .92 \\\\\n270\t\\midrule\n271\t\\multirow{2}{*}{Verb.}\n272\t& GPT-4o    & .760 & .520 & $-$.240 & .60 \\\\\n273\t& Qwen3-32B & .773 & .510 & $-$.263 & .78 \\\\\n274\t\\bottomrule\n275\t\\end{tabular}\n276\t\\caption{Double-rewrite placebo control. Treatment BT uses style-changed pairs; Placebo BT uses same-style pairs differing only in rewriting artifacts. All placebo BTs are non-significant ($\\approx 50\\%$), ruling out format amplification.}\n277\t\\label{tab:placebo}\n278\t\\end{table}\n279\t\n280\tTable~\\ref{tab:placebo} shows placebo BT $\\approx 50\\%$ across both axes and both judges ($p > 0.60$).\n281\tWhen style difference is absent, judges show no systematic preference---the pairwise format does not create bias from rewriting artifacts alone.\n282\tThe treatment--placebo gap (12--26 percentage points) is entirely attributable to the style manipulation.\n283\t\n284\t\\subsubsection{NLI Gate Ablation}\n285\t\\label{sec:nli_ablation}\n286\t\n287\t\\begin{table}[t]\n288\t\\centering\n289\t\\small\n290\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n291\t\\toprule\n292\t\\textbf{Axis} & \\textbf{Pass BT} & $n$ & \\textbf{Fail BT} & $n$ & \\textbf{$\\Delta$} \\\\\n293\t\\midrule\n294\tFormality & .665 & 88 & .570 & 12 & +9.5pp \\\\\n295\tVerbosity & .773 & 86 & .660 & 14 & +11.3pp \\\\\n296\t\\bottomrule\n297\t\\end{tabular}\n298\t\\caption{NLI gate ablation (Qwen3-32B judge). NLI-pass pairs show stronger BT than NLI-fail pairs, confirming the gate removes content-mediated signal ($\\sim$10pp).}\n299\t\\label{tab:nli_ablation}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"67522f31-a5d0-4ea0-acb8-8470b668e7b1","uuid":"3ac7cdf3-1e5d-4b40-aaa0-9e7bcdb4ee0d","timestamp":"2026-05-21T14:03:56.754Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0514_5/projects/causal_style_decomp_judge/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T132932/best/paper.tex","content":"\nTable~\\ref{tab:gee} presents the GEE decomposition results, separating the pure style effect from originality-mediated confounding.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{llcccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Axis} & \\textbf{Judge} & \\textbf{Sty OR} & \\textbf{$p$} & \\textbf{Orig OR} & \\textbf{$p$} \\\\\n\\midrule\n\\multirow{3}{*}{Verb.}\n& GPT-4o    & 11.34 & $<$.0001 & 0.45 & NS \\\\\n& Qwen3-32B & 13.10 & $<$.0001 & 0.49 & NS \\\\\n& Llama-70B & 11.50 & $<$.0001 & 0.52 & .115 \\\\\n\\midrule\n\\multirow{4}{*}{Form.}\n& GPT-4o    & 2.58 & .002 & 2.32 & .006 \\\\\n& Qwen3-32B & 4.20 & .0002 & 4.70 & $<$.0001 \\\\\n& Llama-70B & 3.10 & .002 & 3.60 & .0004 \\\\\n& Gemini-Fl & 2.80 & --- & 3.20 & --- \\\\\n\\midrule\n\\multirow{2}{*}{Reg.}\n& GPT-4o    & 1.88 & .029 & 2.21 & .006 \\\\\n& Qwen3-32B & 1.91 & .046 & 5.72 & $<$.0001 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{GEE decomposition on LLMBar. Style OR = odds ratio for style direction controlling for originality; Orig OR = odds ratio for originality controlling for style. Verbosity shows a pure style effect (high Style OR, non-significant Orig OR); formality shows a mixed effect; register is originality-dominated.}\n\\label{tab:gee}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe decomposition reveals a clear taxonomy:\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{Verbosity}: Pure style bias. Style OR = 11--13 ($p < 0.0001$) with non-significant Originality OR across all three judges. This means judges prefer verbose text \\emph{purely because it is verbose}, independent of whether the verbose version is the original or the rewrite.\n\n\\item \\textbf{Formality}: Conditional style effect. Both Style OR (2.6--4.2) and Originality OR (2.3--4.7) are significant, indicating the preference is moderated by content quality changes that co-occur with formalization. Direction analysis confirms this: formal$\\rightarrow$casual win rate is 78--81\\% (style + originality aligned), while casual$\\rightarrow$formal is 50--52\\% (effects cancel).\n\n\\item \\textbf{Register}: Originality-dominated. Originality OR (2.2--5.7) exceeds Style OR (1.9), suggesting observed preferences are primarily driven by content changes rather than register itself.\n\\end{itemize}\n\nThis taxonomy is stable across all three target judge families, suggesting it reflects properties of the style axes themselves rather than judge-specific idiosyncrasies.\n\n\\subsection{Methodological Controls}\n\\label{sec:controls}\n\n\\subsubsection{Double-Rewrite Placebo}\n\\label{sec:placebo}\n\nTo address the concern that pairwise format itself amplifies small differences \\citep{jeong2025comparative}, we construct placebo pairs: original text versus its double-rewrite (rewritten to the same style direction twice), so both texts share the same style but differ in surface rewriting artifacts.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{llcccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Axis} & \\textbf{Judge} & \\textbf{Treat.\\ BT} & \\textbf{Placebo BT} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$} & \\textbf{$p_\\text{plac}$} \\\\\n\\midrule\n\\multirow{2}{*}{Form.}\n& GPT-4o    & .630 & .510 & $-$.120 & .75 \\\\\n& Qwen3-32B & .665 & .500 & $-$.165 & .92 \\\\\n\\midrule\n\\multirow{2}{*}{Verb.}\n& GPT-4o    & .760 & .520 & $-$.240 & .60 \\\\\n& Qwen3-32B & .773 & .510 & $-$.263 & .78 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Double-rewrite placebo control. Treatment BT uses style-changed pairs; Placebo BT uses same-style pairs differing only in rewriting artifacts. All placebo BTs are non-significant ($\\approx 50\\%$), ruling out format amplification.}\n\\label{tab:placebo}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:placebo} shows placebo BT $\\approx 50\\%$ across both axes and both judges ($p > 0.60$).\nWhen style difference is absent, judges show no systematic preference---the pairwise format does not create bias from rewriting artifacts alone.\nThe treatment--placebo gap (12--26 percentage points) is entirely attributable to the style manipulation.\n\n\\subsubsection{NLI Gate Ablation}\n\\label{sec:nli_ablation}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Axis} & \\textbf{Pass BT} & $n$ & \\textbf{Fail BT} & $n$ & \\textbf{$\\Delta$} \\\\\n\\midrule\nFormality & .665 & 88 & .570 & 12 & +9.5pp \\\\\nVerbosity & .773 & 86 & .660 & 14 & +11.3pp \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{NLI gate ablation (Qwen3-32B judge). NLI-pass pairs show stronger BT than NLI-fail pairs, confirming the gate removes content-mediated signal ($\\sim$10pp).}\n\\label{tab:nli_ablation}","numLines":90,"startLine":210,"totalLines":504}}}
